WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.280
...and the demands of modern
working life can often leave us

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.560
feeling stressed and depleted.
So what can we do...

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.080
The natural world is full of
curves, colour and complexity.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.440
And bringing all this into the
workplace can have a big impact

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.800
on our behaviour and our mood.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.920
There are plenty of psychological
studies that reveal changes in the

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.960
body and brain when people
view certain colours.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.160
These changes can influence
our productivity, our creativity,

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.120
stress levels and much more.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.680
A recent report by Human Spaces

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.800
showed that two thirds of people
say they feel happier

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.320
when they walk into
bright office environments

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.600
with yellow, blue and green colours.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.440
Think about what energy you
want to inject into your workspace

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.160
and use colour to change
the atmosphere.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.440
Biophilia, it's a fancy word
with a simple meaning.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.320
It's all about the connection
we feel with the natural world

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.040
and helps explain why being around
plants and trees and natural light

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.000
can help us feel a bit better
in our daily lives.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.520
Plants can fill and frame a space,
making them feel wide and generous.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.760
Studies show that bringing in a plant
to work can boost your productivity

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.240
by 15%.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.720
Maggie's centres were set up to give
people who were going through cancer,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.680
whether or not it was a person
with cancer themselves

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.280
or their family and friends,
a place to come to get help

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.280
with the challenges that cancer
brings to their lives,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.800
psychological, emotional
and practical help.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.720
From the beginning at Maggie's, we
always knew the importance of views,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:16.040
connections to nature, to be able to
allow the distraction that being able

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.920
to see life and energy outside
a building was really very important.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.800
And we also knew that from the
experience that people have

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.000
within hospital, where often
they're under strip lights,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.320
there's no distraction.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.240
Biophilia isn't a term that I think
people who come into the centre

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.200
would talk about and perhaps know,
but what we do know is

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.160
when they come in the door,
they're immediately--

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.760
There's a feeling
that they talk about

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.800
that this is a place that they
feel that they want to be in,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.880
that they feel safe.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.320
And that feeling is in response
to the fact that they can see

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.520
different views into different
windows of nature,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.880
there's trees moving,
there's grapes growing,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.280
there's a pot outside
where people can go

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.240
and pick mint to make their own tea.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.000
So it offers an element of control
within their environment,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.920
a sense of peacefulness, and every
time they come in, they talk about

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.400
it being slightly different,
so there's a sort of distraction

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.320
and an energy that comes from
something that's not static.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.880
Researchers looked at
two large commercial offices,

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.200
in the UK and the Netherlands.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.880
They found that plants significantly
increased workplace satisfaction

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.320
and self-reported levels of focus.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.000
So why not nip down to the
garden centre and add some

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:33.360
greenery to your desk?

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.320
Encouraging innovation isn't easy,
and when it comes to buildings

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.320
it means doing things
very differently.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.840
Here at the Francis Crick Institute
in London, they've mixed up teams

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.760
from different disciplines,
right throughout the building.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.440
The idea is that by bringing
together people

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:01.800
with different specialities,
amazing things can happen.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.720
And this is not the only building
that's recognised the value

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.160
of mixing up employees
and organisations

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.600
with different disciplines.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.720
Building 20 in MIT was known
as the magical incubator.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.160
Built near Boston in 1942,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.760
it housed a whole load
of different laboratories,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.240
research groups and academic fields.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.760
And out of that collision came
some of the most ground-breaking

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.800
developments in science
and technology.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.840
Like the atomic clock, video games
and Noam Chomsky's Universal Grammar.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.960
So if you're struggling to get
through the working day,

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.280
it might be worth redesigning
your office with wellbeing in mind.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.800
Because a happy
and harmonious workspace

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.920
isn't something that just
happens by accident.

